  "overview": "The Holden Torana (derived from an Aboriginal word meaning 'to fly') is one of Australian motorsport's greatest legends. Starting in 1967 as a modified Vauxhall Viva four-cylinder compact (HB), the Torana evolved into a giant-killing six-cylinder weapon in the LC and LJ series, highlighted by Peter Brock's rainy 1972 Bathurst 500 victory in the triple-carburettor LJ GTR XU-1. The LH and LX generations made Australian automotive history as the only car platform engineered to accommodate 4-, 6-, and 8-cylinder engines, culminating in the Bathurst-dominating SL/R 5000 and A9X option pack.",
  "australian_market_notes": "In 1969, Holden chief engineer George Roberts and designer Leo Pruneau stretched the LC Torana's nose by four inches to package the full-size Holden Red six-cylinder engine into a lightweight compact body, creating the GTR and Harry Firth's GTR XU-1. In 1974, the all-Australian LH series debuted, offering exceptional interior packaging and the thunderous 5.0L SL/R 5000 and L34 homologation packages. In 1976, the LX introduced the stunning three-door Hatchback body style and the immortal A9X option pack (rear disc brakes, Salisbury axle, rear-facing bonnet scoop), which Peter Brock and Jim Richards famously piloted to a crushing six-lap victory at the 1979 Bathurst 1000, setting a lap record on the final lap.",
